APX.gb = {"country":{"name":"United Kingdom","tagline":"Chalk, climate change and Champagne grapes: the country that invented the idea of sparkling wine is now making it seriously.","classification":"Four PDOs: English Wine and Welsh Wine (grapes 100 percent from England or Wales, vineyards below 220 m, quality sparkling limited to Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Pinot Meunier, Pinot Noir Précoce, Pinot Blanc and Pinot Gris), Darnibole (Bacchus from a single Cornish valley) and Sussex (2022, the first regional PDO, with at least 15 months on lees for sparkling). Two PGIs, English Regional Wine and Welsh Regional Wine, allow hybrids such as Seyval Blanc. WineGB's 'Great British Classic Method' hallmark covers traditional-method PDO wines."},"regions":[{"name":"South East England","slug":"south-east-england","tagline":"Kent, Sussex and Surrey: chalk downs and the heart of English sparkling wine.","ids":["gb-english-wine","gb-sussex"]},{"name":"South West England","slug":"south-west-england","tagline":"Cornish valleys, Devon slate and the Darnibole PDO.","ids":["gb-darnibole"]},{"name":"Wales","slug":"wales","tagline":"A small but fast-growing wine country with its own PDO.","ids":["gb-welsh-wine"]}],"items":{"gb-darnibole":{"name":"Darnibole","cat":"PDO","region":"South West England","created":"2017 (EU reg. 19 May 2017)","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Bacchus","summary":"Darnibole is a single valley-side vineyard of Camel Valley, near Bodmin in Cornwall, registered as a PDO in 2017 for still Bacchus. The rules cap yields at 56 hl/ha and require Bacchus grown on the site's slate soils. It was Britain's first single-vineyard PDO and a statement that English still wine could express place.","terroir":"Slate soils on a sheltered, south-facing slope above the River Camel.","producers":["Camel Valley"],"nerd_note":"The name is the old Cornish field name for the site.","rslug":"south-west-england"},"gb-english-wine":{"name":"English Wine","cat":"PDO","region":"South East England","created":"2011 (EU reg. 29 December 2011; UK scheme 2021)","area_ha":4490,"styles":["sparkling","white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Pinot Meunier, Pinot Noir Précoce, Pinot Blanc, Pinot Gris for sparkling; many vinifera varieties for still wine","summary":"The English Wine PDO covers wine made entirely from grapes grown in England in vineyards no higher than 220 metres, with a maximum yield of 80 hl/ha. Quality sparkling wine may come only from the six Champagne and Burgundy grapes, and still wine from a long list of vinifera varieties but not hybrids (which must use the English Regional Wine PGI). Most of England's best wine carries this label, from Nyetimber and Ridgeview to Chapel Down's Bacchus.","terroir":"Chalk downs, Wealden clay and greensand, Essex clay, Devon and Cornish slate; cool maritime climate.","producers":["Nyetimber","Ridgeview","Gusbourne","Chapel Down","Hambledon","Rathfinny","Wiston","Hattingley Valley","Bolney","Exton Park","Hush Heath","Domaine Evremond","Simpsons","Danbury Ridge"],"nerd_note":"About 91 percent of UK sparkling wine in 2024 was made by the traditional method.","contains":["Sussex","Darnibole"],"rslug":"south-east-england"},"gb-sussex":{"name":"Sussex","cat":"PDO","region":"South East England","created":"2022 (UK GI register, 5 July 2022)","area_ha":1132,"styles":["sparkling","white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Pinot Meunier and other classic varieties","summary":"Sussex became the UK's first regional PDO in July 2022 after a campaign by producers including Ridgeview, Wiston, Rathfinny and Bolney. It covers East and West Sussex, with about 1,130 hectares of vines, and sets stricter rules than the English Wine PDO, including at least 15 months on the lees for sparkling wine. The South Downs chalk ridge, Wealden greensand and clay give the county a range of sites, from Rathfinny's chalk bowl near the sea to Nyetimber's greensand at West Chiltington.","terroir":"Chalk of the South Downs, greensand and Wealden clay; maritime climate close to the Channel.","producers":["Ridgeview","Wiston","Rathfinny","Bolney","Nyetimber","Breaky Bottom","Busi Jacobsohn","Tinwood"],"nerd_note":"Breaky Bottom, in a fold of the Downs near Lewes, has made sparkling Seyval Blanc since the 1970s, one of the oldest wineries in Sussex.","rslug":"south-east-england"},"gb-welsh-wine":{"name":"Welsh Wine","cat":"PDO","region":"Wales","created":"2011 (EU reg. 29 December 2011)","area_ha":91,"styles":["white","sparkling","red","rose"],"grapes":"Vinifera varieties; classic varieties for sparkling","summary":"The Welsh Wine PDO mirrors the English rules for grapes grown entirely in Wales. With about 91 hectares in 2025, Wales is small but growing, with vineyards mainly in Monmouthshire and the Vale of Glamorgan.","terroir":"Varied soils on sheltered slopes in south-east Wales; wetter and cooler than south-east England.","producers":["Ancre Hill","Llanerch","White Castle","Llaethliw"],"nerd_note":"Ancre Hill's sparkling wine won a top award at an international sparkling-wine competition in Italy in 2012, a breakthrough for Welsh wine.","rslug":"wales"}}};
